You usually don't want to change the inputs of a function...

```suggestion
bubbleSort = (inputArr) => {
    const outputArr = [...inputArr];
    const len = outputArr.length;
    let swapped;
    do {
        swapped = false;
        for (let i = 0; i < len; i++) {
            if (outputArr[i] > outputArr[i + 1]) {
                const tmp = outputArr[i];
                outputArr[i] = outputArr[i + 1];
                outputArr[i + 1] = tmp;
                swapped = true;
            }
        }
    } while (swapped);
    return outputArr;
};
```

Hey @skatcat31 👋 

I used arr.length because takeRight works by slicing from the right ~ ( end) to the left

Using array.length assures that everything works as expected

Example 

*Lodash*

```js
// _.takeRight([1, 2, 3], 2) -> [ 2, 3 ]
// _.takeRight([1, 2, 3]) -> [3]
// _.takeRight([1, 2, 3], 0) -> []
```

*My Proposed Snippet*

```js
const takeRight = (arr, n = 1) => arr.slice(arr.length - n, arr.length);
// takeRight([1, 2, 3], 2) -> [ 2, 3 ]
// takeRight([1, 2, 3]) -> [3]
// takeRight([1, 2, 3], 0) -> []
```

*Your Proposed Snippet*

```js
const takeRight = (arr, n = 1) => arr.slice(0-n)
// takeRight([1, 2, 3], 2) -> [ 2, 3 ]
// takeRight([1, 2, 3]) -> [3]
// takeRight([1, 2, 3], 0) -> [ 1, 2, 3 ] FAIL TEST : EXPECTED -> []
```

